  "summary": "Tiny Hebrew script drawn into animal shapes and arabesques filling manuscript margins and modern prints.",
  "description": "Jewish micrography writes the masorah — the scribal notes guarding biblical text accuracy — in letters smaller than a grain, then bends the lines into lions, birds, fish and towers filling the margins of Hebrew Bibles. Developed by Masoretes around the tenth century, it flourished in Spanish, French and Yemenite manuscript schools; legal tradition forbade figurative illustration, so letters themselves became the image. Modern Israeli artists revived the form in framed prints, ketubot and calligraphic panoramas.",
